# What must not reach the build context.
#
# node_modules is the one that matters. The Dockerfile installs from the
# lockfile and then copies web/, so a node_modules built on the host -- which
# `npm install`, per the README's developing section, leaves right there --
# would land on top of `npm ci` and put the host's platform binaries in a linux
# image. The lockfile pinning would still be documented and no longer true.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/

# Rebuilt in the image against a different Python than whatever wrote these.
# The `**/` is load-bearing: unlike .gitignore, a bare `__pycache__` here only
# matches one at the root of the context, not app/__pycache__.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.venv

# The runtime reads its settings from the environment compose passes; a copy
# baked into a layer is only a way for the two to disagree.
.env
.env.example

README.md
.dockerignore
